# 13. Security and Hardening

## 13.1 Run as a Non-Root User

Where supported by the image:

```bash
docker run --rm -it --user 1000:1000 debian:bookworm bash
```

The specified user may not have access to every directory or command.

## 13.2 Read-Only Root Filesystem

```bash
docker run --rm -it --read-only debian:bookworm bash
```

Applications that need to write files may require writable temporary filesystems or mounted volumes.

## 13.3 Drop Linux Capabilities

```bash
docker run --rm -it \
  --cap-drop=ALL \
  debian:bookworm bash
```

Some operations will fail because the container has fewer Linux capabilities.

## 13.4 Prevent Privilege Escalation

```bash
docker run --rm -it \
  --security-opt=no-new-privileges:true \
  debian:bookworm bash
```

Prevents processes in the container from gaining additional privileges through mechanisms such as setuid executables.

## 13.5 Check Image Vulnerabilities

Docker Scout can analyze supported images when the feature is available and configured:

```bash
docker scout quickview debian:bookworm
docker scout cves debian:bookworm
```

Availability and reporting depend on Docker Scout installation, authentication, and service access.

## 13.6 Important Security Practices

* Avoid running containers in privileged mode unless absolutely necessary.
* Do not expose the Docker daemon over an unauthenticated network connection.
* Avoid mounting the Docker socket into untrusted containers.
* Prefer trusted, maintained images.
* Keep images and the Docker Engine updated.
* Use secrets management instead of hardcoding credentials.
* Apply resource limits to workloads that need them.
* Use read-only filesystems and drop unnecessary capabilities where practical.
* Remember that containers share the host kernel; they are not equivalent to full virtual machines.

